<page>
            <exercise>
                <title>Matrix Arithmetic</title>
             <task workspace="1in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The <em>sum</em> of two matrices, denoted <m>A + B</m>, is the matrix whose
                                                entries are the sums of the corresponding entries of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
                                                Compute <m>A +B</m> for each pair of matrices below, when possible. If not possible, explain why.
                        <ol>
                                                    <li>
                                                        <p>
                                                            <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}1&amp;3&amp;-1 \\ -2&amp;2&amp;1 \\ 7&amp;8&amp;-10\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                                                            <m>B = \begin{bmatrix}0&amp;2&amp;-5 \\1&amp;3&amp;-1 \\ -5&amp;2&amp;13\end{bmatrix}</m>
                                                        </p>
                                                    </li>
                        
                                                    <li>
                                                        <p>
                                                            <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}0&amp;5 \\ -2&amp;3 \\ 10&amp;4\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                                                            <m>B = \begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 3 \\ 3 &amp; 5 \\ 1 &amp; -1\end{bmatrix}</m>
                                                        </p>
                                                    </li>
                                                    <li>
                                                        <p>
                                                            <m>\displaystyle \begin{bmatrix}2&amp;1 \\ 1 &amp; 2\end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix}2 \\ 3\end{bmatrix}</m>
                                                        </p>
                                                    </li>
                                                </ol>
                    </p>
                </statement>
             </task>

             <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The number <m>0</m> is called an <em>additive identity</em> because
                                                <m>a + 0 = 0 +a = a</m> for any number <m>a</m>.
                                                Is there an additive identity for <m>2 \times 2</m> matrices?
                                                If so, what is it?
                    </p>
                </statement>
             </task>
            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                       For any number <m>a</m>, the number <m>-a</m> is called an
                                               <em>additive inverse</em> of <m>a</m> because <m>a + (-a) = 0</m>.
                                               Does the matrix
                                               <m>\displaystyle \begin{bmatrix}2&amp;1 \\ 1 &amp; 2\end{bmatrix}</m> have an
                                               additive inverse?
                                               If so, what is it? 
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        If <m>b</m> is any real number, the matrix <m>bA</m> is the matrix obtained by
                                                multiplying every entry of <m>A</m> by the number <m>b</m>.
                                                This is called <em>scalar multiplication</em>.
                                                 Compute <m>bA</m> for each pair <m>b,A</m> below, if possible. If not possible,
                                                explain why.
                                                <ol>
                                                    <li>
                                                        <p>
                                                            <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}1&amp;3&amp;-1 \\ -2&amp;2&amp;1 \\ 7&amp;8&amp;-10\end{bmatrix}, b= 3</m>
                                                        </p>
                                                    </li>
                        
                                                    <li>
                                                        <p>
                                                            <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}0&amp;5 \\ -2&amp;3 \\ 10&amp;4\end{bmatrix}, b = -5</m>
                                                        </p>
                                                    </li>
                                                </ol>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                    Does the set of all real matrices form a vector space over <m>\R</m>? Explain why or why not.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
          
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise>
        <title>Matrix Multiplication</title>
        <task workspace="6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <em>Matrix/Vector:</em>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="6in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    <em>Matrix/Matrix:</em>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="2in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find <m>A \cdot B</m> and <m>B \cdot A</m>, when possible, for each pair of
                                        matrices below.
                                        If the product is not possible, explain why.
                                        <ol>
                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 3 &amp; -1 \\ -2 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 \\ 7 &amp; 8 &amp; 2\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                                                    <m>B = \begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 2 &amp; -5 \\1 &amp; 3 &amp; -1 \\ -5 &amp; 2 &amp; -3\end{bmatrix}</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>
                    
                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}0&amp;5 \\ -2&amp;3 \\ 10&amp;4\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                                                    <m>B = \begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 3 &amp; 3\\ 3 &amp; 5 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>
                    
                                            <li>
                                                <p>
                                                    <m>A = \begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 \\ 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0 &amp; 0\end{bmatrix}</m>,
                                                    <m>B = \begin{bmatrix}-1&amp;5 \\ 6&amp;10\end{bmatrix}</m>
                                                </p>
                                            </li>
                                        </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>
        
            
        </exercise>
</page>
